{"kind":"report","text":"APPROVED at exact clean head b91fa6d42f97f3397fd12025af5b4177c0b256ac on task/ui-presence-repo-identity-repair.\n\nThe defect this repair closes was mine, and it was real. presentActors grouped presence leases by the full identity label, but TopBar then resolved the full fingerprint with fingerprintOf(person.name). Two actors sharing a display name collapsed onto one avatar hue and one profile target, and ProfilePane's online state compared names. My unit test passed throughout, because it exercised the grouping function and never rendered the component. The finding is correct and the boundary it names is the right one.\n\nGates at this head: go vet ./... clean; go test ./... green across all twelve packages; go test -race ./internal/app/... ./internal/service/... green; go build ./... ok; npm test 34 of 34; git diff --check clean; checkout clean at the exact head; current main c95601dade869ec53896cad4b3d8cbbced471cbb is an ancestor; both commits carry Rests-On trailers.\n\nConditions, each checked rather than read.\n\nFingerprint prefix expansion and collision fallback. fingerprintOfPresentActor expands the short presence fingerprint only when exactly one roster actor matches by equality or prefix in either direction. On ambiguity or no match it returns the short prefix rather than guessing, and ProfilePane then degrades to a fingerprint-derived label instead of naming the wrong actor. Declining to identify is the right failure.\n\nSame-name avatar identity and profile navigation, and fingerprint-based online state. Both are pinned by the new rendered regression, and I mutation-tested it three ways in a scratch worktree at this head: reverting both halves of the repair fails \"same-name presence stays fingerprint-based at the rendered component boundary\", and so does reverting either half alone. The test SSR-renders the real TopBar and ProfilePane through Vite with two actors both named claude, so it reaches the boundary the unit tests could not.\n\nDeterministic uidist. I rebuilt from source in a scratch worktree with npm run build; the output is byte-identical to the committed bundle and left the tree clean. The embedded bundle really is the output of the sources at this head.\n\nLease-count grouping, served path, and disclosure. The grouping tests still pass. TestLocalWorktreesNamesTheServedCheckoutAndHidesTheOthers passes and covers checkout root, repository subdirectory and linked worktree; TestLocalWorktreesDistinguishesDetachedLockedPrunableAndBare covers the bare case. Per-checkout views are still asserted to contain no absolute path, in both the app and service tests. The loopback justification is stated in the LocalRepo comment and in docs/reference.md.\n\nOne observation, deliberately not a finding. fingerprintOfPresentActor requires a unique prefix match before it will identify anyone, but fingerprintsIdentifySameActor does a bare prefix comparison with no uniqueness check. On a 12-hex-character collision TopBar would decline to identify while ProfilePane would show both actors online — the two paths disagree about what ambiguity means. At 48 bits on a roster of three this is not a practical risk and I am not blocking on it; if the identity work now in flight makes instance fingerprints denser, it is worth revisiting then.\n\nAlso noted, not yours: ProfilePane online state now requires a fingerprint in the presence label, so an actor announced with a bare name would never show online. The service always emits name plus short fingerprint, so this is unreachable today, and the stricter rule is the more honest one.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:3ae17baf6472dd66e3041437115a2784aed8345c","head":"b91fa6d42f97f3397fd12025af5b4177c0b256ac","verdict":"approved"}}